      "narrative": "D-Orbit is a Series C space and aerospace company that has raised $166M to date. The valuation analysis draws on a peer universe of 3 private-market comparables in the same sector and stage (Sierra Space at $8.0B, Varda Space Industries at $1.5B, and Skyryse at $1.1B), alongside a public sector benchmark of 5 traded companies with a median price-to-sales multiple of 23.9x.\n\nTwo methods prove applicable. The stage-progression approach, using total capital raised as a proxy baseline, applies a 1.7x step-up multiplier typical of Series C advancement, yielding $282M. The peer-median method anchors to the three same-stage, same-sector comparables, which cluster at a $1.5B median (range: $1.1B\u2013$8.0B). A public-comp revenue multiple approach cannot be applied because D-Orbit's revenue is not disclosed. These two methods triangulate to a median implied valuation of $891M, with a low bound of $240M (weighted toward the stage-progression result) and a high bound of $1.7B (weighted toward peer medians).\n\nThe analysis supports a valuation range of $240M to $1.7B, with a central estimate near $891M. Key uncertainties include the wide dispersion within the peer set\u2014Sierra Space's $8.0B valuation sits well above Skyryse's $1.1B\u2014and the absence of revenue-based anchoring. Stage-progression assumes linear capital efficiency; peer medians assume comparable market positions and growth trajectories.",
