      "narrative": "Fractile, a Series B semiconductor company founded by Walter Goodwin, is evaluated against a comparable universe of 11 peers (3 public, 8 private) in the chips and semiconductors sector. The target has raised $220M to date. Relevant private peers include Positron AI ($1.0B, Series B), d-Matrix ($2.0B, Series C), Etched ($5.0B, Series C), SambaNova Systems ($11.0B, Series A), and DEEPX ($529M, Series C).\n\nTwo valuation methods triangulate the fair value range. The stage-progression method applies a 1.9x step-up multiplier typical of Series B advancement, yielding $418M from the $220M capital raised baseline. The peer-median method uses the median valuation of five same-sector, same/adjacent-stage comparables, producing $2.0B (range: $529M\u2013$11.0B). The 1.9x multiplier reflects incremental value creation from Series A to Series B, while the peer median captures market pricing for later-stage, higher-growth chip companies in the sample.\n\nThe triangulated analysis supports a valuation range of $355M (low) to $2.3B (high), with a median of $1.2B. Key uncertainties include stage-specific growth velocity relative to the peer set\u2014particularly the wide dispersion from DEEPX ($529M) to SambaNova ($11.0B)\u2014and whether Fractile's technology, market position, and unit economics cluster toward the lower-valuation or upper-valuation comparable.",
