      "narrative": "Telexistence, a Series B robotics and manufacturing company founded by Jin Tomioka and Ken Tomoyose, has raised $170M+ to date. The valuation analysis draws from a comp universe of nine private robotics firms and one public peer, with primary reliance on four same-sector, same/adjacent-stage companies: Bedrock Robotics ($1.8B Series B), Dexterity ($1.6B Series B), Hadrian ($1.6B Series C), and Nimble Robotics ($1.0B Series C).\n\nTwo triangulation methods were applied. The stage-progression method, using total raised as a proxy for current valuation, applies a typical 1.9x Series B step-up multiplier to $170M, yielding $323M. The peer-median method anchors to the four comparable firms, deriving a $1.6B median valuation with a range of $1.0B\u2013$1.8B.\n\nThe two methods produce a wide range: $275M (low) to $1.9B (high), with a median of $974M. This disparity reflects fundamental uncertainty about Telexistence's capital efficiency relative to peer companies. The peer-median approach likely carries greater weight given direct market comparability, but the gap between stage-progression and peer valuations warrants scrutiny into whether Telexistence's capital deployment, technology maturity, or commercial traction materially differs from the comp set. Key uncertainties include revenue run-rate, customer concentration, and burn trajectory relative to Bedrock and Dexterity.",
